\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Signed copies of the oriented spin module}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Let #{n=2m\ge2} and let #{V=S\otimes F} carry the self-adjoint Clifford directions and a self-adjoint involution #{\Gamma} anticommuting with them. Write #{C=\ui^m\psI} for the ordered volume grading of \ref{fdip-009X}; the pseudoscalar #{\psI} is the product of the directions and records their common orientation. Then #{V} splits orthogonally into #{2^m}-dimensional copies of the same Clifford representation. On each copy #{\Gamma=C} or #{\Gamma=-C}. The number of positive copies minus negative copies is #{s=2^{-m}\trace(C\Gamma)}. In scalar links the \vocabk{Wilson kernel}{fdip-0153} with real mass shift acts identically on these copies, so its regular half-signature for the supplied grading is #{s} times the regular half-signature on one copy with grading #{C}.}

\proof{\p{The grading commutes with the even product defining #{C}. Thus #{Q=C\Gamma} is a self-adjoint involution commuting with all Clifford directions. The #{m} self-adjoint involutions #{\ui\varepsilon_{2j-1}\varepsilon_{2j}} commute. The direction #{\varepsilon_{2j-1}} flips the #{j}-th sign and preserves every other sign. Choose an orthonormal basis diagonalizing #{Q} on the simultaneous all-positive subspace. Apply ordered products of the flips to each basis vector. The resulting vectors exhaust orthonormal bases of all sign spaces, since the flips are unitary bijections. For each starting vector the span of its #{2^m} images is stable under every odd-numbered generator by reordering flips, and under the even-numbered generators by the displayed pair involutions. Their actions in the resulting bases are independent of the starting vector. The eigenvalue of #{Q} stays fixed along all flips, giving the stated trace and grading. Scalar transports preserve each copy. Changing #{C} to #{-C} interchanges the positive and negative parts of its Hermitian kernel. Dimensions add over the orthogonal copies.}}
